WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.420
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.800
1: Defining Dharma.

3
00:00:09.689 --> 00:00:14.080
Dharma: A word almost no one can define
without a lot of hand-waving,

4
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.160
and almost everyone uses anyway.

5
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.440
At its root, it means something very
plain, "to hold";

6
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.080
the interesting thing is how far this
plain idea reaches.

7
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.280
The word is everywhere, once you start to
notice it.

8
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.320
There is a yoga studio in nearly every
city called Dharma-something,

9
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.960
its name set in a tasteful sans-serif over
a lotus.

10
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.300
Jack Kerouac wrote The Dharma Bums.

11
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.800
A science-fiction series built a whole
mystery around

12
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.900
a sinister research outfit called the
Dharma Initiative,

13
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.440
and nobody in the audience needed the word
explained,

14
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.680
because we all carry a vague sense of what
it conjures:

15
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.540
something Eastern, something spiritual,

16
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.280
something to do with inner peace

17
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.080
and good intentions and candles and
possibly incense.

18
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.660
The word arrived in English wearing
oriental robes.

19
00:01:05.988 --> 00:01:08.820
It sounds like a soft gong in a quiet
room.

20
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.980
But now try to say what it means: not the
mood it gives off, but the actual meaning.

21
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.520
Most of us, pressed,

22
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.700
manage something like "your destiny,"

23
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.540
or "doing the right thing," or "Buddhist
stuff";

24
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.240
and then the fog rolls in.

25
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.660
It is a word you can use comfortably for
years and never once be able to define.

26
00:01:29.494 --> 00:01:32.120
For me, that is an odd thing for a word to
be,

27
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.060
and it is exactly what made me want to
chase it down.

28
00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:37.840
Because a word that everyone gestures at

29
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.500
and no one can pin is usually hiding
something,

30
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.180
and dharma, it turns out, is hiding quite
a lot.

31
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:47.980
"That which holds"

32
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.220
Strip a word back far enough,

33
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.240
and you reach the moment before it meant
anything abstract,

34
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.640
back when it still meant a physical thing
you could do with your hands.

35
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.900
For dharma, that moment is the Sanskrit
root dhṛ,

36
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.060
and what it means is plain and sturdy:

37
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.160
to hold, to support, to sustain, to keep
from falling.

38
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.200
Philologists trace it further back

39
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.940
to a Proto-Indo-European root (the
reconstructed ancestor of languages ranging

40
00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.960
from Hindi to English) that conveyed the
same sense of holding firm.

41
00:02:20.328 --> 00:02:25.200
You can hear the family resemblance in an
unexpected cousin: the Latin firmus,

42
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.600
"stable, strong," which comes down into
English as the word firm.

43
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.820
Strip away three thousand years of
religion and philosophy,

44
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.020
and dharma means, more or less, that which
holds.

45
00:02:38.693 --> 00:02:42.420
So the word we imagine as wispy and
otherworldly is,

46
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.040
at its root, the word for a prop, the beam
that keeps the roof up.

47
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.620
In the oldest Vedic texts, the hymns of
the Rigveda,

48
00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.600
it names something close to the order of
the cosmos itself,

49
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.920
the deep structure that holds reality
together

50
00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.620
and keeps it from collapsing back into
chaos.

51
00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.300
From there, the story is usually told as a
smooth broadening,

52
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.480
out from the cosmos to society to the
single soul.

53
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.640
The philology tells it differently, and
better.

54
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.980
For centuries after the hymns, dharma was
a minor technical word,

55
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.900
at home in the rituals of kingship and
barely present in the very texts

56
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.300
where a great ethical concept ought to
blossom.

57
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.180
What made it central, on Patrick
Olivelle's reconstruction,

58
00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.820
was an act of appropriation:

59
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.320
the Buddha borrowed this royal word to
name his new teaching,

60
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.660
the emperor Aśoka carved it across India
in his edicts,

61
00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.940
and the Brahmanical schools answered with
an entire expert science of dharma.

62
00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.740
Only then does the word come to cover the
order of society,

63
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.920
the conduct of a single person, and the
path to liberation.

64
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.280
A linguist would file the mechanics under
ordinary semantic change;

65
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.540
the direction was anything but ordinary.

66
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.800
And the metaphor underneath never changes,

67
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:57.120
whether what is being held is the stars or
a single human life.

68
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.420
In one Jain usage the word even names a
literal physical substance,

69
00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.320
the invisible medium that makes motion
possible.

70
00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.900
Carried east and then west, it turns into
"Buddhism,

71
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.960
the Dharma" on a paperback spine, and
then,

72
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:14.280
in our own century,

73
00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.140
into the "secular dharma" of writers

74
00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.320
who keep the practice and quietly drop the
metaphysics.

75
00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.240
Each of those shifts was a real event in
the life of the word:

76
00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.700
lawful in its mechanics,

77
00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.200
unpredictable in its direction, three
thousand years of meaning on the move.

78
00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.020
A word that has travelled that far is not
going

79
00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.700
to sit still for a tidy one-line
definition.

80
00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:37.860
So, if a dharma is "that

81
00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.800
which holds," then the question "what is a
dharma?"

82
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.780
becomes a question about a function, not a
faith.

83
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.020
We are no longer asking which doctrine is
true.

84
00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:52.420
We are asking: what holds a person, or a
people, together under the weight of fear,

85
00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.240
conflict, temptation, and sheer confusion
about how to live?

86
00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:00.640
Every human group, we will see, grows
something to do that holding.

87
00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.520
We are going to spend eight parts looking
at

88
00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.820
the astonishing variety of those
somethings.

89
00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.400
There is no "The Dharma"

90
00:05:13.071 --> 00:05:17.940
If the first oddity was that so wispy a
word rests on so solid a root,

91
00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.980
the second undoes the way the word reached
us.

92
00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:26.300
In English we tend to say "the Dharma,"
with a definite article and a capital D,

93
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.660
as though it named one specific thing,
usually the teachings of Buddhism.

94
00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:35.980
That usage is real, but it is also late,
narrow, and mostly a Western convenience.

95
00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.420
Go back to the traditions that actually
gave rise to the word,

96
00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:44.140
and the tidy singular dissolves into a
bewildering plurality.

97
00:05:46.437 --> 00:05:51.000
In classical Indian thought, there is
svadharma, your own dharma,

98
00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:54.560
the right way of living that belongs
specifically to your role,

99
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.260
station, and stage of life.

100
00:05:56.873 --> 00:06:00.960
The duty of a soldier is to fight; the
duty of a monk is to refrain;

101
00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.260
and these are not two applications of one
rule but two genuinely different dharmas,

102
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.380
each correct for the person whose life it
holds.

103
00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:13.080
Push deeper into the technical Buddhist
philosophy called abhidharma,

104
00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.420
and the word splits again: there,

105
00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.220
"dharmas," in the plural,

106
00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.160
are the tiniest momentary flickers of
experience out of

107
00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.700
which a passing moment is built.

108
00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:27.380
Not atoms, the tradition is quick to
insist, not little grains of mind-stuff:

109
00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.680
but events, each arising and gone with its
conditions.

110
00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:35.880
One word is being asked to mean cosmic
order, and a person's specific duty,

111
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.860
and the fleeting events of consciousness,
all at once.

112
00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.400
No single neat definition can stretch
across all of that without tearing.

113
00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.540
This is not a flaw in my research; it is
the central fact.

114
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.240
There is no single The Dharma, in the way
there is a single Mount Everest.

115
00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.000
There are dharmas, plural: many of them,
suited to many different lives and worlds.

116
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.240
The moment this is accepted,

117
00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.200
a door opens that the rest of this book
can walk through.

118
00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.200
If a dharma is simply a culture's
worked-out answer to "how should we live,

119
00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:10.340
and what holds us together

120
00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:13.720
while we do," then dharmas are not the
property of the East,

121
00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.020
or of religion, or of antiquity.

122
00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.360
A secular philosophy can be a dharma.

123
00:07:19.837 --> 00:07:22.620
A people's unwritten code can be a dharma.

124
00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.700
So, for that matter, can the great
monotheisms,

125
00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.600
and the ethics of a profession, and the
half-conscious creed of a subculture.

126
00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.620
None of them sits automatically above the
others.

127
00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.820
That refusal to rank them in advance is
the stance I take throughout:

128
00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:41.840
secular and sacred, written and spoken,
all treated as equally real candidates.

129
00:07:42.494 --> 00:07:46.340
It falls out directly from the plurality
the word has carried all along.

130
00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.500
And an important word of housekeeping,
because it governs everything that follows.

131
00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.460
From here on, the word "dharma" is simply
a placeholder.

132
00:07:56.832 --> 00:07:59.020
The thing this book is about is the
concept:

133
00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:00.720
the answer to how-should-we-live

134
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.600
that every culture builds and names in its
own tongue,

135
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.920
or builds and never thinks to name at all.

136
00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.940
Ubuntu is not a translation of dharma, nor
ma'at, nor the Tao;

137
00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:14.300
each is a people's own word for their own
instance of the thing.

138
00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.380
I keep the Sanskrit label because, of all
the candidates,

139
00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:21.840
it is the one whose root says most plainly
what the thing does.

140
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.480
Where this project traces the word's
history,

141
00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.800
it is studying a specimen, not consulting
an owner.

142
00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.320
Why everyone has (at least) one

143
00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.880
If dharmas are this varied, and nobody
coordinated them,

144
00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:41.840
why do they turn up everywhere?

145
00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:45.880
Why does every human society, left to
itself long enough,

146
00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:49.100
grow some worked-out answer to how its
people should live,

147
00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.480
and to what holds them together while they
do?

148
00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.360
You might expect, given how different
cultures look on the surface,

149
00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:57.780
that some peoples would simply do without.

150
00:08:58.800 --> 00:08:59.940
But none do.

151
00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:04.660
The reason is one of the most robust
findings in the science of human behaviour.

152
00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.480
Start with the problem every group has to
solve.

153
00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:13.440
Together, people accomplish vastly more
than they manage alone,

154
00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:16.140
but every act of cooperation leaves an
opening:

155
00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:20.660
the cheat, the free-rider, the person who
pockets the benefit and dodges the cost.

156
00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.040
A group with no defence against
exploitation gets eaten from the inside.

157
00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:30.120
Our species' answer, shaped over a very
long time through both genes and culture,

158
00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.460
was a comprehensive toolkit for holding
cooperation together:

159
00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:39.160
reciprocity, reputation, gratitude, guilt,
a keen eye for unfairness,

160
00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:42.820
and a powerful instinct to learn and
enforce the local rules.

161
00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.900
Morality, in this light, is not a coat of
paint over a selfish animal;

162
00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.580
it is the social technology that lets the
animal cooperate at scale

163
00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.100
without being destroyed by its own cheats.

164
00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.640
And we have been at it for an
extraordinarily long time.

165
00:09:59.042 --> 00:10:03.360
For at least fifteen million years our
Hominidae ancestors lived in social groups,

166
00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:06.660
which means that for fifteen million years
the most important

167
00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:09.020
and most dangerous feature of any
individual's

168
00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:11.200
world was other members of the same
species:

169
00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.520
allies to be kept, rivals to be handled,

170
00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.080
cheats to be caught before they bled the
group dry.

171
00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:21.020
The physician and social scientist
Nicholas Christakis argues

172
00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:24.640
that this endless social pressure pressed
a kind of "social suite"

173
00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:26.040
into the human grain:

174
00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:29.320
a reliable bundle of tendencies toward
cooperation,

175
00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:32.780
friendship, in-group loyalty, and learning
from one another.

176
00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.120
It surfaces in every society because it
was shaped long

177
00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.660
before any of those societies existed.

178
00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.440
A dharma, on this reading, is never
written onto a blank slate.

179
00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.980
It is a culture's particular way of
organising, naming,

180
00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:50.960
and fine-tuning a set of social instincts
the species already arrived with.

181
00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:56.980
The evidence that this is universal is
strong, though it requires careful handling.

182
00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.660
The anthropologist Donald Brown assembled
a catalogue of

183
00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:04.480
"human universals" (features found in
every documented society

184
00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.240
with no known exceptions),

185
00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:11.160
and reciprocity, fairness, and a sense of
right and wrong sit squarely on it.

186
00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:16.160
More recently the researcher Oliver Scott
Curry tested the idea directly,

187
00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:19.340
combing the ethnographic records of sixty
different societies

188
00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:21.260
for seven cooperative rules:

189
00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.620
help your family, help your group, return
favours, be brave,

190
00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:30.880
defer to your superiors, divide things
fairly, and respect others' property.

191
00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.700
No single society showed all seven, and
his critics,

192
00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:39.460
who include the anthropologists who staged
a formal debate against him,

193
00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.400
have picked hard at the counting.

194
00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:45.560
But of the several hundred moral
judgements his team coded,

195
00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:47.780
all but one pointed the same way,

196
00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:52.660
and in no society that anyone has yet
examined do the rules run in reverse.

197
00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:56.360
"None was found" is not the same as "there
are none";

198
00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:00.280
still, as a first pass over the species,
it is striking.

199
00:12:00.895 --> 00:12:03.600
The content of morality varies enormously;

200
00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:07.820
but the cooperative spine, so far as
anyone has looked, does not.

201
00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:12.840
And it is the spine, to be exact, that the
evidence shows to be universal:

202
00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.220
the moral machinery itself, not any full
architecture built upon it.

203
00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:21.880
How far a people elaborates that machinery
into something comprehensive,

204
00:12:21.964 --> 00:12:23.780
named, and examined --

205
00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.980
a dharma in the strong sense I will mark
out shortly --

206
00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.340
varies from case to case, and is a matter
of degree rather than kind.

207
00:12:33.341 --> 00:12:35.180
The trick to holding "universal"

208
00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:38.820
and "wildly varied" together at once comes
from biology.

209
00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:42.220
Niko Tinbergen, who studied animal
behaviour,

210
00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:45.380
taught that among the questions you can
ask about any trait,

211
00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:49.180
two stand sharply apart: what is it for,
and how is it built?

212
00:12:49.932 --> 00:12:53.780
The evolutionary biologist David Sloan
Wilson applies exactly

213
00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:55.440
that split to morality,

214
00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.480
and it resolves our puzzle cleanly.

215
00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.980
The function of a dharma is nearly always
the same:

216
00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:04.000
to sustain cooperation and hold the group
together.

217
00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:06.060
But the mechanism that delivers

218
00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:09.600
that function can be almost anything: a
sense of duty,

219
00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.400
a fear of divine punishment, a code of
honour,

220
00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:16.520
the Indian idea of dharma, the southern
African idea of Ubuntu.

221
00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.280
One job, many tools.

222
00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:21.940
That is why dharma-shaped frameworks keep

223
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.380
being reinvented by people who have never
met:

224
00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:28.420
they are local solutions to a problem that
every human community faces.

225
00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:32.860
Written across the sky

226
00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:39.000
You can even see the same impulse manifest
on the scale of the cosmos.

227
00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:44.540
Many great civilisations, often independently,
reached for a grand idea of order,

228
00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:48.340
a single principle fusing the heavens,
society, and right conduct.

229
00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:52.260
India had ṛta, the deep rightness of
things,

230
00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:55.120
a word the philologists prefer to render
as "truth".

231
00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:59.780
Ancient Egypt had ma'at, truth and balance
set against chaos,

232
00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.080
weighed against your heart after death.

233
00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:08.000
The Zoroastrians of Persia had asha, so
close a cousin of ṛta that the two are,

234
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.520
in effect, the same word in different
mouths;

235
00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:13.380
and in the oldest poetry the two peoples
share,

236
00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:18.020
reaching back some four thousand years,
what one did to that truth was hold it up.

237
00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:23.100
The upholding of ṛta is among the most
ancient formulas we can recover,

238
00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:26.020
and the verb in it is dharma's own root.

239
00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:31.980
The Greeks had logos, the rational order
of things; the Chinese had the Tao,

240
00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:35.820
the Way; the Romans and the Christians
after them had natural law.

241
00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.840
The cosmologies could hardly be more
different.

242
00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:43.940
The underlying intuition reads the same
every time: that reality has a grain,

243
00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.900
and that the good life means going with it
rather than against it.

244
00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:51.000
It is the intuition from which the very
word dharma was built.

245
00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:54.280
That which holds, written across the sky.

246
00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:58.920
Those grand orders are not all the same
kind of thing, though,

247
00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:02.320
and one difference among them matters a
great deal for where we are heading.

248
00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:06.760
Most of them -- ṛta, ma'at, the Tao,
natural law --

249
00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:09.040
treat the order as something discovered,

250
00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:14.260
a structure already woven into reality
that we are obliged to find and obey.

251
00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:19.780
The Greeks, characteristically, also kept
a word for the opposite idea: nomos,

252
00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.180
the order that is made,

253
00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:25.720
the conventions and laws a society agrees
on and could have arranged otherwise.

254
00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.820
Dharma sits in a revealing spot between
the two,

255
00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:31.300
though not for the reason I expected to
find.

256
00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:34.100
The tidy story, the one I began with,

257
00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:38.100
has the Indian traditions splitting the
job in half: ṛta above,

258
00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.580
naming the impersonal order of the cosmos,

259
00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:44.040
dharma below, applying it to a particular
human life.

260
00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:47.480
Read the philologists and that staircase
wobbles:

261
00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:50.860
the two words rarely so much as appear
together in the old texts,

262
00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:54.800
and the neat division of labour turns out
to be largely a modern gloss.

263
00:15:56.660 --> 00:16:00.960
What seems to have happened is stranger
and, for this book, more useful.

264
00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:04.680
The old truth-word died out of the
language altogether;

265
00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:08.040
and dharma, the king's word for what a
throne upholds,

266
00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:13.120
was borrowed: by the Buddha, to name his
teaching; by the emperor Aśoka,

267
00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:15.020
for the ideology of an empire;

268
00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.100
by nineteenth-century Hindu reformers, for
a universal ethic;

269
00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.740
by twenty-first-century secularisers,

270
00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:25.400
for a practice with the metaphysics set
gently to one side.

271
00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:29.080
Dharma travels well not because it was
built loose,

272
00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.920
but because it has been so often and so
successfully re-founded.

273
00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:36.880
Nor is it unique in this: even natural
law,

274
00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:39.060
woven though it was into the mind of God,

275
00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:43.620
was eventually secularised by jurists who
kept the law and lost the lawgiver;

276
00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.940
Aquinas himself had left a lower storey
lit by natural reason.

277
00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:51.420
What dharma has is a longer record of
removals.

278
00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:57.240
None of which demotes the upper storey:
for most of the word's history,

279
00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:00.980
and for millions of practitioners now, the
cosmology is the point.

280
00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:03.940
But it does mean that a phrase like
"secular dharma"

281
00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:06.720
is not the contradiction it first appears
to be.

282
00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.820
It is the newest borrowing in a very old
series,

283
00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:14.120
and the series itself is the strongest
evidence we will meet that dharmas are made.

284
00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:17.560
It is also worth being clear about what
did the travelling.

285
00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:22.420
The concept needs no vehicle: every people
reinvents it unprompted,

286
00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:24.340
as this whole section has been showing.

287
00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:29.480
What travelled was a word, one culture's
label for the thing,

288
00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:32.600
passed from hand to hand and re-founded at
each stop.

289
00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:38.220
This book borrows it in exactly that
spirit, and holds it just as lightly.

290
00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:43.980
Custom, law, and the things a dharma is
not

291
00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:49.720
By now you may be feeling a reasonable
suspicion.

292
00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:55.040
If a dharma can be a religion, a
philosophy, or a people's unwritten code,

293
00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.500
and if the impulse behind it is universal,

294
00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:00.920
then hasn't the word swollen to mean
simply "human values"?

295
00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:04.440
A word that means everything means
nothing.

296
00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:09.340
If dharma is going to bear the weight of
this whole book, the word needs a boundary:

297
00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:13.180
some way of distinguishing what is a
dharma from what merely looks like one.

298
00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:17.400
The boundary is not about content.

299
00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.900
There is no single belief or rule you can
point

300
00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:23.020
to and say that is what makes it a dharma.

301
00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.540
It is about a combination of features,

302
00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:28.200
and the cleanest version I found runs to
four.

303
00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:33.880
The first is comprehensiveness: a dharma
governs a whole life,

304
00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.700
or a whole domain of life, rather than a
single slice of behaviour.

305
00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:42.800
It has something to say about work,
speech, money, family, authority,

306
00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:46.900
suffering, and death; it is an
architecture, not a single rule.

307
00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:53.480
The second is that it is identity-constituting:
a dharma binds you from the inside,

308
00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:57.860
by conviction and identity rather than
from the outside by force.

309
00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:03.260
It also works to remake you, telling you
who to become and offering the practices --

310
00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:06.740
habits, disciplines, rituals -- to get you
there.

311
00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:12.740
The third is an orientation toward the
good: it is about how one ought to live.

312
00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:17.000
The classical Indian tradition drew that
line itself, between dharma,

313
00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:21.840
the ethical path, and artha, the business
of getting and keeping worldly advantage.

314
00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.520
And the fourth is reflective
self-awareness,

315
00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:30.300
the one that will matter most once we meet
dharmas being built on purpose:

316
00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:33.460
a dharma can be held up and known as a
path,

317
00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:39.080
something a person or a people can name,
examine, and deliberately choose or reform,

318
00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:42.300
rather than merely absorbed as the way
things obviously are.

319
00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:47.980
Two pieces of fine print belong here,
before the test is put to work;

320
00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:51.480
both are fingerprints of the kind I defend
at this part's end.

321
00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:53.320
First, the scoring.

322
00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:58.720
"Oriented toward the good" means the good
as the dharma's own carriers conceive it:

323
00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:03.000
the feature marks the register of ought
off from the register of advantage,

324
00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:07.160
the line the Indian tradition itself drew
between dharma and artha.

325
00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:11.900
It does not ask whether the conception is
in fact any good;

326
00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:14.640
that question gets a part of its own much
later,

327
00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:17.340
and keeping it out of the definition is
deliberate,

328
00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:21.480
because Part 6 will need to call some
genuinely dark things "dharmas"

329
00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:23.900
without the definition ejecting them
first.

330
00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:27.600
Likewise the fourth feature names a
capacity:

331
00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:31.620
most dharmas are absorbed half-consciously
and never feel chosen,

332
00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.780
and what marks them is that they can be
held up and examined.

333
00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:36.940
Second, the provenance.

334
00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:41.340
The research note behind this part carries
a different fourth feature,

335
00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:44.420
a transformative telos -- the "who to
become" --

336
00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:46.980
which this version folds into the second
feature,

337
00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:50.460
freeing the fourth slot for reflective
self-awareness precisely

338
00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.500
because the end of this book will need it.

339
00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:57.120
That is a choice, made by me, for a
purpose you can now see coming,

340
00:20:57.140 --> 00:21:01.100
and I flag it so that no one following the
links mistakes it for a finding.

341
00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.420
Run the everyday impostors against those
previous four features,

342
00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:08.400
and you can see why they fall short.

343
00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:14.000
Custom, the way a village does things,
supplies rules,

344
00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:18.920
but it is usually inherited without
reflection and carries no transformative aim;

345
00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:22.240
it tells you what is done, not what is
good and why.

346
00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:28.900
Law is comprehensive and powerful, but it
binds the wrong way: from the outside,

347
00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:33.360
by coercion, enforceable by punishment and
abolishable by a vote.

348
00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:39.240
Repeal a statute, and it is simply gone; a
dharma is not undone so easily,

349
00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:42.120
because it lives in conviction rather than
on a statute book.

350
00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:47.720
Etiquette governs only the polished
surface of conduct (which fork,

351
00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:50.960
which form of address) and asks nothing of
your soul.

352
00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:55.560
The hardest case is ideology, which, like
a dharma,

353
00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:59.780
is comprehensive and fuses values with a
story about the world;

354
00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:03.840
and plenty of ideologies sincerely offer
their adherents transformation,

355
00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:07.540
sacrifice, discipline, an identity to grow
into.

356
00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:10.620
Nor can the difference be purity of
motive,

357
00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:13.240
since dharmas have served power often
enough:

358
00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:16.400
the word itself began in the rituals of
kingship,

359
00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:18.660
and Aśoka carved it across an empire.

360
00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:21.880
What separates them is centre of gravity.

361
00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:26.380
An ideology is organised first around
political mobilisation,

362
00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:28.860
the winning and wielding of collective
power;

363
00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:33.040
a dharma is organised first around the
formation of persons,

364
00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:37.000
and carries that distinctive layer of
inner practice (meditation,

365
00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:40.460
prayer, training) that an ideology can do
without.

366
00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:44.380
The line is real, but it is the blurriest
of the four,

367
00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.760
and traffic crosses it in both directions:

368
00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:50.900
a dharma curdles into an ideology when it
is captured by power,

369
00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:52.180
and an ideology starts

370
00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:56.360
to look like a dharma the moment it begins
telling its followers who to become.

371
00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:00.920
You may have noticed that this test has
soft edges,

372
00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:03.180
and those edges are part of the design.

373
00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:07.200
Membership in a category like this is a
matter of degree rather

374
00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:10.280
than a clean yes-or-no. Just

375
00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:14.260
as a magpie is somehow a more obvious bird
than a penguin or an emu,

376
00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:18.580
some dharmas are textbook cases and others
sit out near the blurry rim.

377
00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:23.060
Buddhism, Stoicism, and Ubuntu carry all
four features in full,

378
00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:25.200
and the test admits them without a wobble.

379
00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:30.820
But what about environmentalism, with its
values, its disciplines of consumption,

380
00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:33.220
its story about humanity and nature,

381
00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:37.180
and its converts who genuinely reorganise
their lives around it?

382
00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:41.840
What about the ethos of a demanding
profession, medicine or the military,

383
00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:46.200
that tells its members who to become and
drills them until they are it?

384
00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:50.860
What about a subculture like punk, with
its code and its refusals?

385
00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:53.400
Run the four features over each of these

386
00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:56.260
and you will find them scoring two or
three out of four,

387
00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:57.860
hovering right at the threshold.

388
00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:01.940
I do not think there is a hidden fact that
settles such cases,

389
00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:04.780
and chasing one would lead us away from
what matters.

390
00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:08.140
The features are a tool for seeing family
resemblance,

391
00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.280
not a border guard stamping passports.

392
00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:15.480
What they let us do is compare: to notice
that a profession, a religion,

393
00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:19.060
and a peasant movement can be doing
recognisably the same work,

394
00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:22.260
holding a life together in recognisably
the same way,

395
00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:26.060
even when one of them would never dream of
calling itself a dharma.

396
00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:31.320
A definition is a tool, not a truth

397
00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:36.400
I have just handed you a four-part
definition,

398
00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:38.460
and I want to immediately complicate it,

399
00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:40.480
because the most interesting thing I
learned in

400
00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:43.440
defining dharma is something about
definition itself.

401
00:24:45.280 --> 00:24:48.860
Words like this one resist being pinned,
and not by accident.

402
00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:53.740
The philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein made
the point with the humble word "game".

403
00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:58.600
Try to find the single feature common to
all games: board games,

404
00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:03.280
ball games, solitaire, a child spinning in
a circle, the Olympics.

405
00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:08.080
Competition cannot be the common thread,
because solitaire has none;

406
00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:11.420
skill cannot be it, because snakes and
ladders needs none;

407
00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:14.320
fun cannot be it, because the professional
has come

408
00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:16.380
to dread the game he plays for a living.

409
00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.540
There is no single feature that runs the
whole length.

410
00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:22.060
Instead there is, in his phrase,

411
00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:25.800
"a complicated network of similarities
overlapping and criss-crossing":

412
00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:27.260
a family resemblance.

413
00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:33.060
Such a concept is held together not like a
single thread but like a rope:

414
00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:36.380
its strength comes from many short fibres
twisted together,

415
00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:38.540
no one of which runs end to end.

416
00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:40.520
Dharma is a rope.

417
00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:45.060
(Though one fibre, it must be said, runs
remarkably far:

418
00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:48.540
the scholars who catalogue the word's
Buddhist senses find

419
00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:51.660
nearly all of them still carrying some
scent of support,

420
00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:52.980
of holding-in-place.

421
00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:57.360
A rope, then; but a rope with one long
strand near its core.)

422
00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:00.180
That is why the question scholars of the
word learned

423
00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:03.280
to ask was never "what is dharma" (a
demand

424
00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:04.480
for the one true essence)

425
00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:08.860
but "what makes something a dharma," a
question about family resemblance.

426
00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:12.820
There is a further wrinkle.

427
00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:18.400
The philosopher WB Gallie noticed that
certain words (art, democracy,

428
00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:22.580
social justice) are more than vague: they
are essentially contested.

429
00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:25.640
The argument about what they really mean
is not a problem

430
00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:29.080
to be solved but a permanent feature of
the concepts themselves.

431
00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:31.480
People will still be disagreeing about the

432
00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:33.940
true meaning of justice in a thousand
years;

433
00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.820
that endless disagreement is part of what
the word is.

434
00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:40.540
Dharma belongs in that company with one
caution attached.

435
00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:43.600
Gallie's own test asks whether the rival

436
00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:46.300
users are at least contesting the same
prize,

437
00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:48.500
heirs of one acknowledged exemplar,

438
00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:50.160
and the Vedic ritualist,

439
00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:54.820
the Abhidharma scholastic and the secular
meditator are not obviously doing that.

440
00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:59.960
Within a single tradition, dharma is
contested exactly as Gallie described;

441
00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:01.860
across the whole plural field,

442
00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:05.500
it is less a disputed trophy than the rope
of the last paragraph.

443
00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:10.980
So when I offer you those four features, I
am offering a tool:

444
00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:13.680
a deliberately sharpened version of a
fuzzy word,

445
00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:15.280
cut to a particular purpose,

446
00:27:15.320 --> 00:27:17.780
which is to let us compare very different
ways of living

447
00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:19.720
without collapsing them into one.

448
00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:24.940
A definition, on this view, is less like a
true statement and more like a map,

449
00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:27.520
useful precisely because it leaves things
out,

450
00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:30.260
and judged by whether it takes you where
you need to go.

451
00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:34.840
There is a third and harder version of the
objection,

452
00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:37.080
and it deserves to be met at full
strength,

453
00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:40.860
because it comes from inside the
discipline this whole project leans on.

454
00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:45.580
The anthropologist Talal Asad argued, in a
famous critique,

455
00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:48.780
that "there cannot be a universal
definition of religion,"

456
00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:51.020
and not merely because the word is fuzzy:

457
00:27:51.040 --> 00:27:53.760
because any such definition "is itself the

458
00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:56.300
historical product of discursive
processes."

459
00:27:56.840 --> 00:27:58.600
The very urge to lift a single,

460
00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:00.580
functional category out of the world's
ways

461
00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:02.740
of living grew out of a specific Christian

462
00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.000
and then secular European history,

463
00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:09.100
and it has served administration and
empire at least as often as understanding.

464
00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:13.040
Every word of that critique applies to
what I am doing with dharma.

465
00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:15.940
Part 6 will show how the nineteenth
century

466
00:28:15.940 --> 00:28:19.000
manufactured the very containers called
"world religions"

467
00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:21.380
and then forgot they were manufactured;

468
00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:25.000
on Asad's terms, I am manufacturing
another.

469
00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:30.660
The only response I have found is to
disarm the thing that makes it lethal,

470
00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:32.160
which is concealment.

471
00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:36.160
A definition does its damage when it poses
as a discovery.

472
00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:40.120
Mine is offered as a tool with the
toolmaker's fingerprints left on it:

473
00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:42.180
chosen by a particular person,

474
00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:44.800
out of a particular history, for a stated
purpose,

475
00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:48.320
to be judged by the work it does and
discarded where it fails.

476
00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:52.460
And candour, I should admit, buys less
than it seems to:

477
00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:55.220
confessing a genealogy does little to
disarm it.

478
00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:59.740
What actually answers Asad is that a tool
of this kind can be tested.

479
00:29:00.670 --> 00:29:02.840
Its claims can fail against the world,

480
00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:07.680
and one failure above all wants watching
for: define a dharma by the work it does,

481
00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:10.440
and you will find one in every society you
look at,

482
00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:13.100
because the finding is packed into the
definition.

483
00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:17.620
That is why the universality claimed two
sections ago was offered as evidence,

484
00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:19.940
counted societies and their published
critics,

485
00:29:19.960 --> 00:29:22.380
and not as a consequence of my four
features.

486
00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:26.000
What cannot be claimed, after Asad, is
innocence.

487
00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:29.420
What can be claimed is authorship, on the
record,

488
00:29:29.420 --> 00:29:31.160
and a willingness to be marked wrong.

489
00:29:31.640 --> 00:29:33.140
And the question he forces,

490
00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:37.180
is not "is the definition true," but: for
what work, for whom,

491
00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:39.340
do we need to stabilise this word at all,

492
00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:42.280
and what plurality do we put at risk by
stabilising it?

493
00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:46.200
This is the method of the whole
undertaking.

494
00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:50.580
Faced with a drifting, contested,
three-thousand-year-old word,

495
00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:54.760
the tempting move is to plant a flag and
declare what dharma really means.

496
00:29:55.240 --> 00:29:58.460
The appropriate move, the one I will try
to keep faith with,

497
00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:01.720
is to map its uses instead: to lay out
many dharmas,

498
00:30:01.820 --> 00:30:04.220
with their contexts and their tensions
intact,

499
00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:07.280
and resist flattening them into a single
official version.

500
00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:11.340
That will matter enormously when, near the
end of this journey,

501
00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:14.660
we ask whether a machine could be taught
human ethics at all,

502
00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:17.080
or whether teaching it would simply crush
the plural

503
00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:20.080
into a single average and call the result
the truth.

504
00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:24.520
Two questions I'm leaving open

505
00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:29.440
By now, two objections are probably
forming,

506
00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:31.860
and rather than answer them now I am going

507
00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:34.300
to name them and deliberately leave them
standing,

508
00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:37.900
because they are the twin destinations the
whole argument is heading for.

509
00:30:39.560 --> 00:30:41.480
The first is about authority.

510
00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:45.540
I have defined a dharma as something that
binds you from the inside,

511
00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:47.900
by conviction rather than by force,

512
00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:51.200
but I have also said a dharma need not
rest on any god.

513
00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:55.640
So a sharp question follows: without a
divine lawgiver behind it,

514
00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:57.780
can a dharma really bind you at all?

515
00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:00.620
Or is a godless dharma just a set of
personal

516
00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:02.960
preferences wearing the costume of duty?

517
00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.480
It is the oldest question in ethics.

518
00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:09.640
Plato was already turning it over in a
dialogue called the Euthyphro two

519
00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:11.240
and a half thousand years ago,

520
00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:15.600
and the trap he set there for the divine
lawgiver has never been cleanly escaped.

521
00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:17.680
I think there is a real answer.

522
00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:21.200
But we will face it properly when we reach
the modern,

523
00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:24.640
secular, god-haunted world several parts
from now.

524
00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:28.100
The second is about judgement.

525
00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:33.100
If dharmas are genuinely plural and none
automatically sits above the others,

526
00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:34.860
then are they all equally good?

527
00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:39.640
When a dharma is cruel, or cages half its
people, or feeds a death camp,

528
00:31:39.660 --> 00:31:42.800
am I really reduced to shrugging that it
is simply their way?

529
00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:47.220
The worry is serious enough that I do not
want it festering for eight parts:

530
00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:49.160
plural is not the same as anything goes.

531
00:31:50.860 --> 00:31:53.360
To say there is no single true dharma is
not

532
00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:56.100
to say that every dharma is as good as
every other,

533
00:31:56.120 --> 00:32:00.420
any more than admitting there are many good
languages means every sentence is true.

534
00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:03.680
There is a floor, and some dharmas fall
through it.

535
00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:05.980
But building that floor, honestly,

536
00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:09.780
without smuggling in my own culture's
furniture and calling it the universe,

537
00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:13.760
is delicate work, and it belongs at the
end of the road, not here.

538
00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:18.540
The most ordinary thing in the world

539
00:32:20.910 --> 00:32:24.300
So what did I find, going to look for the
meaning under the fog?

540
00:32:25.980 --> 00:32:29.320
I found that the word we treat as exotic
is, at its root,

541
00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:32.300
the plainest word imaginable: that which
holds.

542
00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:37.600
I found that there is no single Dharma but
a teeming plurality of dharmas,

543
00:32:37.780 --> 00:32:41.640
secular and sacred alike, none of them
granted the high ground in advance.

544
00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:45.440
I found that every human society grows
one,

545
00:32:45.500 --> 00:32:48.520
because every human society faces the same
problem of how

546
00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:50.800
to cooperate without being torn apart,

547
00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:53.500
and a dharma is the characteristic human
solution.

548
00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:59.020
And I found that "dharma" is best treated
as a tool: a map we draw for a purpose,

549
00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:02.260
holding the plurality open rather than
freezing it shut.

550
00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:08.740
Which means the thing we have been taught
to see as faraway and faintly mystical is,

551
00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:11.500
in fact, the most ordinary thing in the
world.

552
00:33:12.380 --> 00:33:16.200
You are standing inside a dharma right
now, some inherited,

553
00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:20.320
half-examined account of how to treat
people and what a good life looks like,

554
00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:22.580
whether or not you have ever given it a
name.

555
00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:24.600
Almost everyone is.

556
00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:28.000
The interesting question was never whether
you have a dharma.

557
00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:31.620
It is which one -- or which ones -- where
it came from,

558
00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:35.040
how it got inside you, and whether it
deserves to keep you.

559
00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:40.840
We have a working definition and a plural,
unprivileged field to explore.

560
00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:43.680
The very next question is the obvious one:

561
00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:47.160
if dharmas are made rather than handed
down from the sky,

562
00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:48.880
where do they actually come from?

563
00:33:49.380 --> 00:33:52.440
What is it about being human that makes us
build these things,

564
00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:55.040
over and over, wherever we appear?

565
00:33:55.480 --> 00:33:58.700
To answer that, we have to go and watch
one being born:

566
00:33:58.840 --> 00:34:03.380
back past the philosophers and past the
scriptures, to the first crowded cities,

567
00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:06.200
where the ancient social instincts we just
met were made

568
00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:09.240
to hold together more strangers than they
had ever faced.

